I simultaneously participated in evaluation accounts from four proprietary (prop) trading firms using the same computer. However, only Moneta’s account suffers severe system lag (“disk lag”), extreme latency—often exceeding one to two minutes—persistent disconnections, unresponsive order execution, and repeated failed submissions requiring multiple retries.

I reported this issue to Moneta’s support team and provided them with full, timestamped screen recordings documenting the problem—recordings I still retain in full. Yet they continue to insist the issue is solely on my end.

To rule out software-related causes, I tested Moneta’s account on the same computer, using different MT5 platforms (e.g., other brokers’ official MT5 terminals)—and the exact same issues occurred. This clearly confirms it is not an MT5 client issue, nor a problem with my OS, drivers, or local configuration. Crucially, all other prop firms’ accounts—on that identical device, identical network, identical hardware—operate flawlessly, with zero lag, no disconnects, and instant order execution.

When I escalated this to Moneta’s engineering team, every representative dismissed it as “a problem on our side”—refusing to investigate server-side latency, gateway instability, IP routing anomalies, or infrastructure bottlenecks affecting only their platform. I am utterly perplexed: if every other firm works perfectly under identical technical conditions, how can Moneta alone be experiencing such critical, systemic performance failure—unless the root cause lies within their infrastructure?

Even more concerning:

They demanded I delete my public review before even agreeing to look into the issue. I complied—removing the comment—but after nearly one month, they have yet to resolve it—or even acknowledge receipt of my follow-up.
They remain unresponsive and dismissive.
They even demanded I submit the source code of my Expert Advisor (EA) for testing—yet my account has already been suspended by them. Why would I voluntarily share proprietary EA source code with a firm that has already terminated my access, especially when the issue demonstrably occurs even during manual trading (no EA involved)? There is no reasonable, professional, or ethical basis for such a request.


